  "text": "Solution 2's upper bound is unsupported. Its Hall-condition check assumes that at most two colors can be forbidden at every position in a pair, but three neighbors can forbid three colors at both positions 1 and 2. For example, with neighbor permutations arranged as a derangement, Hall's condition fails at v, so the greedy elimination argument does not establish $t(3) \\le 4$.\n\nSolution 1's bound of $2d$ also rests on a loose Hall argument, and its lower-bound construction is only sketched, so I don't trust its value of 6.\n\nSolution 3 cites a general bound $\\chi_l^{\\star}(G) \\le \\mathrm{tw}(G)+2$ and a construction attaining it. I can't verify that citation from memory, and the claimed general bound is suspicious. Its value of 5 for $t(3)$ does match my recollection of published results on list packing of bounded-treewidth graphs, though I am not certain of that recollection. Of the three, it is the only one whose final answer is plausible and not contradicted by a visible error in the argument.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
